title: YARA-L 2.0, hands-on
guide: howto/yara-l.html
promise: Learn YARA-L 2.0 from zero on CloudTrail data: Unified Data Model fields, match windows, two-event joins, and a silent rule with the control that exposes it.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It assumes you have written detection rules or log searches. Reading the rules needs a text editor; running them, a tenant of Google SecOps, Google's platform for searching security logs.

Disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz, which Google owns.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
Nothing was executed: not the rules, not the searches, not the commands. Every rule was checked against Google's YARA-L documentation, field-mapping reference and published rules. Expected results were worked out from the lab file, not captured from Google SecOps.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read it in order. Paste BASH blocks into a terminal, replacing YOUR_ placeholders. Save each whole YARA-L rule from the guide as its rule name plus .yaral; slides show parts. Without a tenant, read each rule against the lab file.

== Set up: the lab file, a test tenant and the CLI
section: 1. Get the lab file, and a tenant to run it in
Step 1's first block writes the lab file; paste it whole, EOF line included. With the gcloud command-line interface (CLI) installed, these install the secops CLI, sign in and load the file into a non-production test tenant. Expect 24 events.
```bash
python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
python3 -m pip install secops
gcloud auth application-default login
secops config set --customer-id "YOUR_INSTANCE_ID" --project-id "YOUR_PROJECT_ID" --region "us"
secops log ingest --type AWS_CLOUDTRAIL --file cloudtrail-lab.jsonl
secops search --query 'metadata.product_name = "AWS CloudTrail"' \
  --start-time 2026-09-15T08:00:00Z --end-time 2026-09-15T12:00:00Z
```

== What YARA-L is and where you meet it
section: A detection language that reads a data model, not your logs
YARA-L 2.0 is Google SecOps' query language. Detection engineers write rules in it, which Google SecOps checks against incoming events, recording a detection each time a rule's pattern holds. Analysts search with it. It is not YARA, the file-scanning language.

== The lab: 24 CloudTrail events and a leaked key
section: Walk-through: five detections on one lab file
The lab file holds 24 CloudTrail events from one morning. A CloudTrail event is a record of one call made to Amazon Web Services (AWS).

A continuous integration user, build-bot, has leaked its access key.

== What someone does with build-bot's key
section: Walk-through: five detections on one lab file
From 203.0.113.50 someone is mostly refused, then creates the user backup-svc, attaches the AWS managed policy AdministratorAccess to it, gives it an access key and, as backup-svc, stops the CloudTrail trail.

Earlier, bob signs in without multi-factor authentication (MFA).

== include: _cloudtrail-lab.deck

== A rule never reads a log as it arrived
section: Rules read UDM events, not log lines
A parser rewrites each log into the Unified Data Model (UDM), shared field names. A rule naming a UDM field your parser leaves empty is accepted, runs and never fires. Before trusting a rule, print each lab line's UDM event:
```bash
secops parser run --log-type AWS_CLOUDTRAIL --logs-file cloudtrail-lab.jsonl
```

== The smallest rule that works
section: Rules read UDM events, not log lines
$trail is an event variable: any event passing every line in the events section, here a StopLogging call. In the condition, $trail means at least one exists. The parser copied CloudTrail's eventName into metadata.product_event_type. Only the lab's 10:09:03 call passes.
```yara-l
rule lab_stop_logging_seen {
  meta:
    description = "Any StopLogging call"
  events:
    $trail.metadata.product_event_type = "StopLogging"
  condition:
    $trail
}
```

== D4: test the result as well as MFA
section: 3. A nested value, and a naive rule that fires too often (D4)
MFA status, additionalEventData.MFAUsed in CloudTrail, lands in extensions.auth.auth_details. Testing only that also catches a failed attempt on alice at 09:20:03, so the fix tests the sign-in result too: only bob remains. Check the last two lines with secops parser run.
```yara-l
  events:
    $login.metadata.product_name = "AWS CloudTrail"
    $login.metadata.event_type = "USER_LOGIN"
    $login.metadata.product_event_type = "ConsoleLogin"
    $login.extensions.auth.auth_details = "MFAUsed: No"
    $login.security_result.severity_details = "Success"
```

== D2: a rule that cannot fire
section: 4. Counting, and a rule that cannot fire (D2)
CloudTrail's errorCode lands in security_result.summary. The placeholder $ip captures each event's address; the match section groups events by it over 10 minutes; #denied counts each group's events. Expected on the lab: nothing. 203.0.113.50 has four AccessDenied events, 192.0.2.20 three.
```yara-l
  events:
    $denied.metadata.product_name = "AWS CloudTrail"
    $denied.security_result.summary = "AccessDenied"
    $denied.principal.ip = $ip
  match:
    $ip over 10m
  condition:
    #denied >= 5
```

== Before you trust a quiet rule, run a control
section: 4. Counting, and a rule that cannot fire (D2)
A control is a search that must find something. This drops the threshold: expect AccessDenied 7, and Client.UnauthorizedOperation 3 for the attacker's Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) refusals. Matching every refusal code, the fix counts 7 for 203.0.113.50 and fires.
```bash
secops stats --start-time 2026-09-15T08:00:00Z --end-time 2026-09-15T12:00:00Z \
  --query 'metadata.product_name = "AWS CloudTrail"
security_result.summary != ""
match:
  security_result.summary
outcome:
  $events = count(metadata.id)
order:
  $events desc'
```

== D3: two events, one new user, ten minutes
section: 5. Two events, one new user, ten minutes (D3)
Two event variables, $create and $key, share the placeholder $new_user; the timestamp test puts creation first. Expected on the lab: backup-svc, keyed after 39 seconds. svc-reporting, which alice keyed 2 hours 37 minutes after creating it, fits no 10-minute window.
```yara-l
    $create.metadata.product_name = "AWS CloudTrail"
    $create.metadata.product_event_type = "CreateUser"
    $create.target.user.user_display_name = $new_user
    $key.metadata.product_name = "AWS CloudTrail"
    $key.metadata.product_event_type = "CreateAccessKey"
    $key.target.user.user_display_name = $new_user
    // the user has to exist before its key does
    $create.metadata.event_timestamp.seconds < $key.metadata.event_timestamp.seconds
  match:
    $new_user over 10m
```

== Test every rule both ways
section: 6. From rule to running detection, tested both ways
This runs secops rule test, which prints each rule's matches, on six whole rules from the guide's steps 2 to 5 (D1 to D4). Check each against its "Expected on the lab" line: what must fire and what must not.
```bash
for r in lab_cloudtrail_logging_tampered lab_mfa_no_naive lab_console_login_without_mfa \
         lab_denied_burst_naive lab_denied_call_burst lab_new_user_given_access_key; do
  echo "== $r"
  secops rule test --file "$r.yaral" \
    --start-time 2026-09-15T08:00:00Z --end-time 2026-09-15T12:00:00Z
done
```

== Exercise: write D5 as a rule
section: Hands-on exercises
Write a rule that fires when the AWS managed policy AdministratorAccess is attached to a user. Which lab event must it catch, and how do you give it a negative case? In the guide, "Show the answer" opens each answer.

== Answer: test target.resource.name
section: Hands-on exercises
The policy's Amazon Resource Name (ARN) lands in target.resource.name. It must catch build-bot attaching it to backup-svc at 10:06:30. The lab has no other attachment, so copy lab line 20 with ReadOnlyAccess instead, ingest it and confirm silence.
```yara-l
  events:
    $attach.metadata.product_name = "AWS CloudTrail"
    $attach.metadata.product_event_type = "AttachUserPolicy"
    $attach.target.resource.name = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess"
```

== Exercise: Google's MFA rule finds nothing
section: Hands-on exercises
You paste Google's aws_console_login_without_mfa rule unchanged. It sets $account_id from additional.fields["recipientAccountId"] and its match section is $account_id over 1h. Tested over the lab's morning it returns nothing, though bob's sign-in is there. Why, and how would you find out?

== Answer: the match section drops empty values
section: Hands-on exercises
The trimmed lab events carry no recipientAccountId, so $account_id is empty, and Google SecOps drops empty values in the match section. Take parts away until bob appears: the match section first, then the older parser's success test, security_result.action = "ALLOW".

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- Run secops parser run on a sample before trusting any field name.
- Google's CloudTrail rules match error codes in security_result.rule_id, not security_result.summary, so they can run and never fire.
- The secops rule validate command proves only that a rule compiles.
